Post-Surgical Asthenia
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Profound generalized functional fatigue post-operation | Complete absence of surgical complications or severe anemia | Gradual self-limiting resolution over weeks

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-018
Reason for Visit: Acute failure to mobilize and sudden severe weakness three weeks post-operation, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, or occult surgical site infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult offspring
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate profound, sudden-onset exhaustion and an inability to stand following recent surgery. Immediate clinical review was mandated to exclude delayed postoperative complications, preventing severe cardiovascular or septic collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had my gallbladder out three weeks ago, but today I couldn't even walk to the kitchen without feeling like my muscles were completely failing.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with profound, generalized fatigue following a laparoscopic cholecystectomy three weeks prior. There is decreased physical stamina completely out of proportion to expected recovery timelines. The patient reports an increased sleep requirement during the day and subjective muscle weakness without focal neurological deficits. There are emotional lability and mild depressive symptoms regarding the lack of progress. The patient notes a mild loss of appetite and difficulty concentrating on complex tasks. Fatigue is severely exacerbated by minor physical exertion, causing a slowed pacing of instrumental activities of daily living.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound due to severe subjective exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver exhaustion and patient frustration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Supine 118/74 mmHg, Standing 110/72 mmHg | HR: Supine 78 bpm, Standing 98 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 84 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, frustrated, and visibly exhausted, laying flat in bed. During the orthostatic vital sign check, there is notable postural lightheadedness and upright palpitations, with immediate relief upon lying supine.
Physical Exam: Surgical incisions are fully healed, clean, dry, and intact with zero signs of surrounding erythema or fluctuance. Abdomen is soft and non-tender. Cardiopulmonary examination is normal. Bilateral lower extremities demonstrate no unilateral swelling, erythema, or calf tenderness (Homan's sign negative). Neurological examination shows symmetric 5/5 motor strength in all extremities despite the patient's subjective feeling of weakness.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is cluttered with medical supplies; limited natural light.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently ceased oral opioid analgesia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Surgical Asthenia - Status: Stable, compounded by mild orthostatic intolerance and deconditioning.
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out clinically due to normal SpO2, clear lung fields, absence of pleuritic chest pain, and lack of unilateral leg swelling.

Surgical Site Infection / Deep Abscess - Ruled out due to completely normalized body temperature, clean incision sites, and lack of focal abdominal pain or rigidity.

Postoperative Anemia - Ruled out as primary acute cause due to normal conjunctival color and lack of resting tachycardia, though requires formal lab correlation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Fall risk when fatigued).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s profound failure to thrive and sudden inability to mobilize post-surgery mandated an urgent after-hours evaluation. Telephone triage accurately identified the high-risk window for deep vein thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, or delayed surgical sepsis. Rigorous physical examination definitively ruled out active infection, severe cardiopulmonary compromise, or deep venous thrombosis. By comprehensively excluding these life-threatening postoperative complications, the assessment safely kept the patient out of the emergency department, providing the necessary clinical reassurance and structured rehabilitation strategies needed to manage this extreme post-surgical deconditioning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, non-infectious nature of the current fatigue. The expected investigation results include a normal complete blood count and comprehensive metabolic panel ruling out acute postoperative complications, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via the regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild physical stamina without triggering severe exhaustion crashes, implement a strict "five minutes on, one hour off" activity rule; adhering to this extremely micro-paced physical activity will slowly rebuild muscle endurance while protecting your healing metabolic pathways.
Home-Based Services: Referral generated for home-based physiotherapy for supervised reconditioning.
DME/Safety: Continue using the shower chair and ensure someone is in the home during mobilization.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days for a complete blood count and metabolic panel.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a sudden high fever, new severe abdominal pain, coughing up blood, or sudden swelling in one leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal hematological review; recovery timelines vary drastically between individuals.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves a prolonged, non-linear return to baseline energy levels, often taking up to three months post-general anesthesia.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if you develop sharp pain in your chest when taking a deep breath, or if your surgical scars suddenly become red, hot, or begin weeping fluid.

